#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prerequisites {#prerequisites}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You should be familiar with [Qiskit patterns](/guides/intro-to-patterns), "
"[transpilation](/guides/transpile), and the [Sampler "
"primitive](/guides/primitives).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Periodic boundary conditions connect the last qubit in a chain to the first."
" If these qubits are far apart on a quantum processor, implementing their "
"interaction requires routing, which can add two-qubit gates and increase "
"circuit depth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Gate cutting replaces a two-qubit gate with a weighted combination of "
"single-qubit operations and measurements. Executing these experiments and "
"combining their results reconstructs expectation values for the original "
"circuit. This can reduce routing overhead, at the cost of additional "
"sampling. See the [circuit cutting "
"explanation](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-"
"cutting/explanation/index.html) for the underlying quasiprobability "
"decomposition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You will first validate this workflow on a six-qubit noiseless simulator. "
"Then you will apply it to a chain of at least 30 qubits on hardware and "
"compare the cut and uncut results. The circuit uses parameters with "
"analytically known expectation values, so the hardware comparison does not "
"require a large statevector simulation.\n"
msgstr ""

